### Scope of the conclusions [429]
427: It shows why the transcript divergence cannot be attributed solely to the\n428: number of releases: preserving the individual observations matters.\n429: \n430: \section{Scope of the conclusions}\n431: The finite theorem supplies a sufficient domain, not a complete classification\n432: of privacy targets. It compares each smaller batch with full batch and does\n433: not order every pair of smaller batches. The sparse cutoff is uniform in\n434: energy, but fixes $E,\Delta,\varepsilon,\delta$. At a fixed dataset size $N$,\n435: the expected batch $EN/T$ eventually falls below one; a joint limit in $N$\n436: or a changing privacy target requires a separate analysis.\n437: \n438: The fixed-rate asymptotic comparison in R\"ais\"a, J\"alk\"o, and\n439: Honkela~\cite[Corollary 5.4]{raisa2024} follows a different path: for fixed\n440: $q>0$, its calibrated raw-noise ratio satisfies\n441: $\sigma(q,T)/(q\sigma(1,T))\to1$. It cannot be specialized to $q=E/T$\n442: without uniformity in the rate. Here the expected participation, rather than\n443: the sampling rate, is fixed.\n444: \n445: All comparisons concern independent Poisson sampling, homogeneous independent\n446: Gaussian noise, and equal-weight averages of fixed clipped contributions.\n447: The common-channel argument requires the query to stay fixed across neighboring\n448: datasets. It does not justify substituting a privately chosen optimization\n449: trajectory into this model.
